# Live-run side store (checkpoint UoDs + state + recorded polls are run artifacts,
# never the corpus; the run LOG is tracked)
runs/*/checkpoints/
runs/*/state.json
runs/*/frontier.json
runs/*/polls.jsonl
runs/*/items.jsonl
runs/*/weather_state.json
runs/*/asks.jsonl
runs/*/STOP

# Live-run output directories (RUN 13, the vault cycle, and every numbered
# run dir after it) — custody boundary: everything a live driver writes
# against a real vault/source (UoDs/chains/journals/side-stores) is
# model-derived from private material and never enters git. Whole-branch
# review (2026-07-19, IMPORTANT 2): the literal `runs/run13/` rule only
# covered one hardcoded number, but `--runs-dir` is a free argument and run
# directories get renumbered across sessions (RUN 14, 15, ... already
# routine) — a future run one `git add -A` away from committing verbatim
# author prose. Generic glob instead: ANY `runs/run<digits...>/` directory
# is ignored wholesale (not just checkpoint subpaths, as above).
#
# Re-review (2026-07-20, NEW-3): the prior comment here claimed the
# top-level aggregate logs (`runs/RUN_*_LOG.md`, `runs/OPERATIONS.md`,
# `runs/EXAMINATION_IV_BRIEFS/`) were spared because of their UPPERCASE
# `RUN_` — FALSE on this repo: `core.ignorecase=true` (the default on a
# case-insensitive filesystem, e.g. macOS/APFS), so git casefolds the
# glob before matching (verified: `runs/run*/` DOES match a hand-made
# `runs/RUN_TEST_DIR/` directory here). The reasoning that actually holds:
# the trailing slash makes this glob directory-only, so it can never match
# a FILE regardless of case (`RUN_12_LOG.md`, `OPERATIONS.md`); and the
# pattern below is now tightened to require digits right after `run`
# (case-insensitively), so `EXAMINATION_IV_BRIEFS/` (doesn't start with
# "run" at all) AND a future `RUN_14_BRIEFS/`-shaped DIRECTORY (starts
# with "run" case-folded, but not followed by a digit) both stay
# unmatched on every platform, not just case-sensitive ones. Already-
# tracked files under earlier `runs/runN/` directories (e.g.
# `runs/run3/filmstrip/*.svg`, `runs/run10/console.txt`) are unaffected —
# .gitignore only screens untracked paths, never untracks what git
# already knows about.
runs/run[0-9]*/

# West-in-kytē E2 sweep output — same custody boundary as above (a synthetic
# vault, but the driver's own working directory is not corpus content). No
# trailing slash (final review, Minor 1): a slash-terminated glob is
# directory-only and would silently miss a console redirect like
# `> runs/west_e2_console.txt` — the exact footgun that left
# runs/run13_console.txt untracked-and-unignored.
runs/west_e2*
